(Regression test: heterogeneous lists must hold and print every element
 correctly. Previously the element type was inferred from the first
 element only, so [1, "two", 3.5] printed a raw pointer and a float's
 bit pattern - silent data corruption. Per-slot runtime type tags fix
 this; booleans print as 1/0, matching homogeneous boolean lists.)

a list called m is [1, "two", 3.5, yes].

(Iteration dispatches per element)
For each item in m, print item.

(Format interpolation dispatches per element)
For each item in m, print "got: {item}".

(1-indexed element access dispatches per element)
Print element 1 of m.
Print element 2 of m.
Print element 3 of m.
Print element 4 of m.

(first/last carry their slot's tag)
Print m's first.
Print m's last.

(Element writes retag their slot)
Set element 1 of m to "one".
Print element 1 of m.
Set element 2 of m to 22.
Print element 2 of m.

(Aliases share the same tagged block)
a list called alias is the m.
For each av in alias, print av.

(Homogeneous lists are untouched by all of this)
a list called nums is [10, 20, 30].
For each n in nums, print n.
a list called names is ["ada", "bob"].
For each nm in names, print nm.
